  "why": "The three series are the same measurement (the framework, 16 MiB upload, 1 MiB chunks, freshly started emulators), but their medians are 0.75 s (experiment a), 0.63 s (b, framework alone) and 0.93 s (b, interleaved), and every pair differs (all Holm p < 0.05). Within each series, upload time changes as the run goes on: Spearman rho +0.71 in a, +0.13 for the framework alone in b and +0.89 interleaved in b; the median of the first third of the trials against the last third is 0.66 s against 0.76 s in a, 0.62 s against 0.63 s alone in b and 0.76 s against 1.03 s interleaved in b. Both arms of the interleaved block slow down as the run goes on: the 16 MiB upload takes 0.76 s then 1.03 s for the framework and 0.48 s then 0.80 s for plain chunked storage (first and last third of the repetitions), so the ratio moves from 1.59 to 1.29; downloads show no such drift. The plain arm deletes its objects after every trial and still slows down, so a growing number of stored objects does not explain it by itself. The cause (emulator internals, disk state or machine state) was not identified. Switching the laptop to AC power and a high-performance plan did not remove the drift. The consequences are that absolute times from different experiments must not be compared, that the ratios of experiment b are averages over a comparison that changes during the run, and that only arms interleaved inside one experiment can be compared at all.",
